      "content": "20|\n21|## Stickyickistan Connections\n22|\n23|This is not a random portal appearing in the wilderness. The crisis is the result of several Stickyickistanian interests pushing against the same dangerous seam in reality.\n24|\n25|- **The FLS Guildhall:** The party accepts the public contract here on [[Nevercough Island]]. The Records Nook contains reports of missing surveyors, strange lights over [[Zooka Mire]], and previous monster incidents in [[The Outback]]. When the party returns, the Guildhall records whether the breach was sealed, who recovered the artifacts, and whether any adventurers came home carrying a curse.\n26|- **N.E.R.D:** N.E.R.D's planar-studies faculty funded Zardan's work and supplied the planar hourglass, survey maps, and measurement instruments. The outpost was intended to be a field station for studying how [[Arcanite]] affects planar boundaries. N.E.R.D wants the party's notes and samples, but its faculty is also quietly worried that the failed experiment may expose the institution to accusations of reckless research.\n27|- **The Seekers of the Vein:** A Seeker survey team identified the unusually rich Arcanite resonance beneath [[Zooka Mire]] and sold the readings to N.E.R.D as a research lead. The Seekers did not knowingly open the breach, but their pressure to keep extracting from [[Mount Zooka]] made the local Weave less stable. If the party recovers intact Arcanite fragments or Githzerai instruments, the Seekers will claim them as survey property.\n28|- **The Forgewright Guild:** The Forgewrights built or maintained some of the Magitech components used in the outpost's warning array. Their engineers want the damaged array returned so they can determine whether the failure was sabotage, bad design, or an impossible interaction between Magitech and Limbo.\n29|- **Zooka Mire and the Zooka Mire Collective:** The outpost sits in the Collective's territory. The Collective regards the breach as a wound in the local cycle, and its sensory network has noticed the slaadi before anyone in the city did. The party may encounter signs that the Collective has been watching the citadel: fungal markers, redirected waterways, or scavengers removing contaminated remains. The Collective will not necessarily help the party, but it may oppose anyone who tries to haul unstable Arcanite or slaad remains through the Mire.\n30|- **The Ashen Shadow Society and ASSCUNT:** The Society's recovery contract is not just for missing researchers. ASSCUNT, directed by [[Kezrin Malvek]], wants dangerous biological and magical components recovered for the [[Ashen Citadel]]. A Bloodhound squad may be sent if the party fails; if the party succeeds, the Society may claim slaad tissue, the control gem, the Steelbeast, or the survey array as contracted property. [[Zara Blackthorn]] can be the unseen authority behind the contract if a named Society representative is useful.\n31|- **The Loot Tavern:** If the party needs an emergency escape route, [[Port Ale]] can return them to the extraplanar tavern. Lutecia can also prepare a teleportation flight back to [[Nevercough Island]], but the party must supply the drink and pay the usual fee.\n32|- **Zahur al-Nasira:** Zahur's palace is a recognizable landmark in Limbo, not a new destination for the adventure. His attention is optional: he might observe the breach as entertaining chaos, but he has no obligation to rescue the party or solve the crisis.\n33|\n34|Zardan's research is useful to several groups for different reasons. N.E.R.D wants knowledge, the Seekers and Forgewrights want control of the Arcanite and Magitech implications, the Zooka Mire Collective wants the breach stopped without further ecological damage, the Free League wants its adventurers alive, and ASSCUNT wants anything valuable enough to harvest. The party decides who receives the truth—and who receives the dangerous things they bring back.\n35|\n36|## Running the Stickyickistan Version\n37|\n38|Use the existing factions to make the adventure feel like a local crisis rather than a generic planar dungeon.\n39|\n40|- **At the Guildhall:** The contract is posted publicly through [[The Rolling Papers]], while a quieter note in [[The Roach Clip]] promises extra payment for intact Arcanite, slaad organs, or working Githzerai devices. The public contract is N.E.R.D's; the private bounty is from ASSCUNT.\n41|- **On the road to Zooka Mire:** The party passes through [[The Outback]] and finds evidence that the [[Zooka Mire Collective]] has already started containing the contamination. Fungal markers and scavenged equipment point toward the citadel without requiring a new guide or settlement.\n42|- **At the citadel:** The failed warning array bears both N.E.R.D cataloguing marks and [[The Forgewright Guild]] components. A successful Investigation or Arcana check can reveal that the array did not simply malfunction: the Arcanite resonance was amplified by the presence of the slaadi.\n43|- **After the breach closes:** The party must decide what to report. A complete report to N.E.R.D may expose the institution's reckless fieldwork; returning the array to the Forgewrights may preserve useful Magitech research; handing over specimens or the control gem to ASSCUNT may create a future containment problem; and leaving contaminated material for the Collective may prevent it from spreading through Stickyickistan.\n44|\n45|These choices do not need to change the core encounters. They give the existing Stickyickistan factions reasons to care about the same scenes and let the consequences continue through later Free League contracts.\n46|\n47|## Chapter 1: Compromised Citadel\n48|\n49|## A Brother's Fate\n50|\n51|You may read from the following text box to describe the Githzerai citadel's first floor to the party:\n52|\n53|> [!Read Aloud]\n54|> You enter a room that seems to serve multiple purposes: racks of basic survival equipment and weapons line one wall, cooking utensils and chests of non-perishable foods occupy a corner, shelves of maps and leather-bound tomes surround meditation mats, and a long table stands in the center. A Githzerai sits alone at this table, his back to you.\n55|\n56|**Githzerai Corpse.** If players choose to approach or investigate the figure, they notice that the body is withered and smells of decay. Deep slashes are gouged in its neck, and a massive hole gapes where its belly should be. A trail of dry blood and intestines sprawls on the table in front of it. A DC 12 Investigation, Perception, or Medicine check reveals that the stomach seems to have burst outward, as if something was violently ejected from it. Struggling to contain his grief, Zardan recognizes this corpse as his brother and, given the evidence, believes that a red slaad killed him.\n57|\n58|### Slaadi, the Terrors from Limbo\n59|\n60|If players don't know what slaadi are, you can have Zardan explain the following.\n61|\n62|Slaadi are large, malevolent, toad-like aberrations that spawned from the chaos of Limbo eons ago. They are extremely dangerous and follow their only instinct, which is to destroy inferior life forms. The Githzerai are committed to eliminating threats posed by these vile abominations, but rarely succeed without significant losses.\n63|\n64|The two types of slaadi reproduce parasitically in an unnatural cycle: a red slaad plants eggs in a living humanoid, which hatch into a blue slaad; conversely, a blue slaad infects a humanoid with a disease called \"chaos phage\" that transforms the afflicted creature into a red slaad.\n65|\n66|Additional slaad lore can be found in MM274.\n67|\n68|## Slaad Encounter\n69|\n70|A staircase leads from the citadel's ground-level floor of operations to the second-story habitation. In this room are five simple beds with chests containing the monks' personal belongings (robes, writing supplies, small wooden humanoid effigies, personal trinkets; nothing of value).\n71|\n72|The four other monks stationed in this outpost are piled in the center of the room. Their bodies are riddled with claw marks, but perceptive characters note that their pallid faces, sweat-covered brows, contorted expressions, and ragged breaths suggest that they are alive, but in excruciating pain. Even more perceptive characters notice that their stomachs are obscenely swollen.\n73|\n74|If a character approaches or tries to investigate, one of the monks feebly lifts his head and implores the party to put an end to their agony, when all of a sudden, the Githzerai's stomachs burst in unison. Four squalling slaadi tadpoles (MM 276) wriggle onto the floor and attack the party.\n75|",
